The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
// Original check_file_record() did not validate rec->used
if (rec->rhdr.sign != NTFS_FILE_SIGNATURE ||
    le32_to_cpu(rec->total) != rs) {
    return false;
}
// In do_action() handlers:
memmove(attr, ..., used - asize - roff);
```
Reason:  
The `check_file_record()` function only validated `rec->total` but not `rec->used`. Journal-replay handlers like `DeleteAttribute` compute `memmove` lengths as `rec->used - <offset>` without bounds. A corrupted `rec->used` smaller than the offset or larger than the record size causes integer underflow or overflow, allowing huge memory copy into a 4KB buffer.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
// Added in check_file_record():
u32 used = le32_to_cpu(rec->used);
// ... after total check:
|| used > rs || used < ao
// ... after attribute loop:
if (used < PtrOffset(rec, attr) + sizeof(attr->type))
    return false;
```
Reason:  
The first addition ensures `rec->used` is within the record bounds (`used <= rs`) and at least covers the attribute offset (`used >= ao`). The second addition guarantees that after walking all attributes, `rec->used` covers the ATTR_END marker, preventing underflow in subsequent `memmove` length computations.

